@Tutorial(time: 15) {
    @Intro(title: "Setting up the game area") {
        In this first part, we're going to set up the game area. Let's get started by
        importing the start assets and setting up the game scene.

        We've prepared a Godot project with the 3D models and sounds we'll use for this
        tutorial, linked in the index page. If you haven't done so yet, you can download
        the archive here:
        [Squash the Creeps assets](https://github.com/godotengine/godot-docs-project-starters/releases/download/latest-4.x/3d%5Fsquash%5Fthe%5Fcreeps%5Fstarter.zip).
    }
    @Section(title: "Importing the start assets") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            Once you downloaded the archive, extract the .zip on your computer. We'll
            then import the starter project into Xogot and take a look at what is
            included.
        }
        @Steps {
            @Step {
                In the Godot Project Manager, tap the *Import* button.

                @Image(source: 01.import_button.webp)
            }
            @Step {
                In the import popup, enter the full path to the freshly created
                directory `3d_squash_the_creeps_starter/`. You can tap the *Browse*
                button on the right to open a file browser and navigate to the
                `project.godot` file the folder contains.

                @Image(source: 02.browse_to_project_folder.webp)
            }
            @Step {
                Tap *Import* to open the project in the editor.

                @Image(source: 03.import_and_edit.webp)
            }
            A window notifying you that the project was generated by an older Godot
            version may appear.

            @Step {
                If that prompt appears, tap *OK* to convert the project to your
                current Godot version.

                @Image(source: import_project_to_4.x_prompt.webp)
            }
            If the project didn't open automatically, open it from your project
            list.

            The start project contains an icon and two folders: `art/` and `fonts/`.
            There, you will find the art assets and music we'll use in the game.

            @Image(source: 04.start_assets.webp)

            There are two 3D models, `player.glb` and `mob.glb`, some materials that
            belong to these models, and a music track.
        }
    }
    @Section(title: "Setting up the playable area") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            We're going to create our main scene with a plain
            [Node](https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/classes/class%5Fnode.html#class-node)
            as its root. Then we'll add a floor that prevents the characters from
            falling, and a directional light so the scene isn't all grey.

            To create static colliders like the floor, walls, or ceilings, you can
            use
            [StaticBody3D](https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/classes/class%5Fstaticbody3d.html#class-staticbody3d)
            nodes. They require
            [CollisionShape3D](https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/classes/class%5Fcollisionshape3d.html#class-collisionshape3d)
            child nodes to define the collision area.
        }
        @Steps {
            @Step {
                In the *Scene* dock, tap the *Add Child Node* button represented by
                a "+" icon in the top-left and double-tap on *Node*. Name the node
                `Main`.

                > Tip: An alternate method to rename the node is to long-press on
                > *Node* and choose *Rename* (or use the F2 shortcut on a hardware
                > keyboard). You can also add a node by pressing Ctrl + A
                > (Cmd + A on macOS).

                @Image(source: 05.main_node.webp)
            }
            @Step {
                Save the scene as `main.tscn` by pressing Ctrl + S (Cmd + S on
                macOS).

                > Important: Saving early and often prevents accidental loss of
                > work if the app closes unexpectedly.

                @Image(source: fix-me.png, alt: "Save the scene as main.tscn")
            }
            @Step {
                With `Main` selected, add a
                [StaticBody3D](https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/classes/class%5Fstaticbody3d.html#class-staticbody3d)
                child node, then a
                [CollisionShape3D](https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/classes/class%5Fcollisionshape3d.html#class-collisionshape3d)
                child of the `StaticBody3D`. Rename the `StaticBody3D` to `Ground`.

                @Image(source: adding_static_body3D.webp)
            }
            Your scene tree should look like this.

            @Image(source: 06.staticbody_node.webp)

            > Note: A warning sign next to the `CollisionShape3D` appears because
            > we haven't defined its shape yet. If you tap the warning icon, a
            > popup appears with more information.

            @Image(source: 07.collision_shape_warning.webp)

            @Step {
                Select the `CollisionShape3D` node, head to the *Inspector* and tap
                the *<empty>* field next to the *Shape* property. Create a new
                *BoxShape3D*.

                > Tip: The box shape is perfect for flat ground and walls. Its
                > thickness makes it reliable to block even fast-moving objects.

                @Image(source: 08.create_box_shape3D.webp)
            }
            A box's wireframe appears in the viewport with three orange dots. You
            can tap and drag these to edit the shape's extents interactively. We
            can also precisely set the size in the inspector.

            @Step {
                Tap on the
                [BoxShape3D](https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/classes/class%5Fboxshape3d.html#class-boxshape3d)
                to expand the resource. Set its *Size* to `60` on the X-axis, `2`
                for the Y-axis, and `60` for the Z-axis.

                @Image(source: 09.box_size.webp)
            }
            Collision shapes are invisible. We need to add a visual floor that goes
            along with it.

            @Step {
                Select the `Ground` node and add a
                [MeshInstance3D](https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/classes/class%5Fmeshinstance3d.html#class-meshinstance3d)
                as its child.

                @Image(source: 10.mesh_instance3d.webp)
            }
            @Step {
                In the *Inspector*, tap on the field next to *Mesh* and create a
                [BoxMesh](https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/classes/class%5Fboxmesh.html#class-boxmesh)
                resource to create a visible box.

                @Image(source: 11.box_mesh.webp)
            }
            @Step {
                Once again, it's too small by default. Tap the box icon to expand
                the resource and set its *Size* to `60`, `2`, and `60`.

                @Image(source: 12.cube_resized.webp)
            }
            You should see a wide grey slab that covers the grid and blue and red
            axes in the viewport.

            We're going to move the ground down so we can see the floor grid. To
            do this, the grid snapping feature can be used.

            > Tip: Grid snapping can be activated in two ways. The first is by
            > tapping the *Use Snap* button in the toolbar (or pressing the Y key
            > on a hardware keyboard). The second is by selecting a node,
            > dragging a handle on the gizmo **then** holding Ctrl (Cmd on
            > macOS) while holding the selected node.

            @Image(source: use_snap.webp)

            @Step {
                Start by setting snapping with your preferred method. Then move the
                `Ground` node using the Y-axis (the green arrow on the gizmo).

                > Note: If you can't see the 3D object manipulator like on the
                > image above, ensure the *Transform Mode* is active in the
                > toolbar above the view.

                @Image(source: move_gizmo_y_axis.webp)
            }
            @Step {
                Move the ground down `1` meter, in order to have a visible editor
                grid. A label in the bottom-left corner of the viewport tells you
                how much you're translating the node.

                > Important: Moving the *Ground* node down moves both children
                > along with it. Ensure you move the *Ground* node, **not** the
                > *MeshInstance3D* or the *CollisionShape3D*.

                Ultimately, `Ground`'s transform.position.y should be -1

                @Image(source: ground_down1meter.webp)
            }
            @Step {
                Let's add a directional light so our scene isn't all grey. Select
                the `Main` node and add a child node
                [DirectionalLight3D](https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/classes/class%5Fdirectionallight3d.html#class-directionallight3d).

                @Image(source: create_directional_light3d.webp)
            }
            @Step {
                Move the `DirectionalLight3D` up by tapping and dragging on the
                manipulator's green arrow, then tap and drag on the red arc to
                rotate it around the X-axis until the ground is lit.

                In the *Inspector*, turn on *Shadow* by tapping the checkbox.

                @Image(source: 16.turn_on_shadows.webp)
            }
            At this point, your project should look like this.

            @Image(source: 17.project_with_light.webp)

            That's our starting point. In the next part, we will work on the
            player scene and base movement.
        }
    }
    @Section(title: "Enabling the onscreen controller") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            We will set up the game to support a wireless gamepad, and we'll
            also turn on Xogot's
            [onscreen virtual controller](https://docs.xogot.com/documentation/xogot/onscreen-controls)
            so players without a physical controller can play directly on
            their iPad with on-screen touch controls.

            The virtual controller emulates hardware gamepad actions, so
            we'll bind the left thumbstick and the A button to our `move_*`
            and `jump` input actions in the next section. For now, we just
            enable the controller and decide which parts are visible.
        }
        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Open the project settings by tapping the **...** toolbar
                button in the upper-right corner of Xogot and tapping
                **Settings**.

                @Image(source: fix-me.png, alt: "The ... toolbar button in the upper-right with the Settings menu open")
            }
            @Step {
                In the left column, scroll down to **Input Devices** and tap
                *Virtual Controller*.

                > Tip: The *Virtual Controller* settings live under *Input
                > Devices*, not under the more familiar *General* tab
                > where the Input Map is.

                @Image(source: fix-me.png, alt: "The Virtual Controller entry in the Input Devices list")
            }
            @Step {
                Toggle **Enable Controller** on. Then turn **off** everything
                except the **Left Thumbstick** and the **A** button.

                For this project we'll use the left thumbstick to move the
                player and the A button to jump — exactly what would
                appear on a physical Xbox-style gamepad.

                > Important: The button names use the **Xbox naming
                > convention** (A, B, X, Y). We'll bind the A button to
                > `jump` in the next section so the on-screen A button
                > jumps the player.

                @Image(source: configure-virtual-controller.png)
            }
            You can dismiss the settings dialog. The onscreen controls will
            automatically appear when you run your project on an iPad and
            hide themselves when a physical controller is connected.

            In the next section we'll set up the input actions and bind
            them to the on-screen controls.
        }
    }
}
